# H2 spin triplet, bond at theta = 90 deg to B (bond in the x-z plane), potential curve E(R).
# Two starts per R: mol (Lz 0, -1 about the B axis) and atm (two magnetically translated H 1s).
# The lower converged E_phys is the ground state at that R. theta = 90: Lange et al., Science 337, 327
# (2012) found the H2 triplet bound perpendicular to B (paramagnetic bonding, B = 0.75-2.25 B0 there); no reference exists at this field.
# R up to 0.60 to see whether this orientation is bound at all.
# Summary: bash summarize.sh results/12_H2_theta90_B425 <2 E(H) from file 10>
COMMON B=425.5 k=10 thresh=1e-6 eprec=1e-9 L=8 qextra=10 h=0.25 tmin=1e-8 tmax_factor=45 dphi=3 inner=3 tolscf=1e-5 rcut=1.0 rtaper=0.1 maxscf=30
R0.2_mol        nelec=2 state=triplet axial=1 ma=0 mb=-1 E0=203.5 E0b=207.7 "atoms=1,-0.1,0,0;1,0.1,0,0"
R0.2_atm        nelec=2 state=triplet axial=0 mloc=0 E0=207 "atoms=1,-0.1,0,0;1,0.1,0,0"
R0.24_mol       nelec=2 state=triplet axial=1 ma=0 mb=-1 E0=203.5 E0b=207.7 "atoms=1,-0.12,0,0;1,0.12,0,0"
R0.24_atm       nelec=2 state=triplet axial=0 mloc=0 E0=207 "atoms=1,-0.12,0,0;1,0.12,0,0"
R0.28_mol       nelec=2 state=triplet axial=1 ma=0 mb=-1 E0=203.5 E0b=207.7 "atoms=1,-0.14,0,0;1,0.14,0,0"
R0.28_atm       nelec=2 state=triplet axial=0 mloc=0 E0=207 "atoms=1,-0.14,0,0;1,0.14,0,0"
